Subject: ksmserver -- launch session apps with fork()+execvp() not exec_blind

ksmserver normally launches session apps (window manager, panel, desktop) via an
async DCOP exec_blind() to tdelauncher.  On substrate the tdelauncher
start_service path does not reliably bring up kdesktop, so launch the command
directly with fork()+execvp() instead (binaries are on $PATH under
/opt/trinity/bin).  Inter-app DCOP itself works (the af_unix SO_PEERCRED fix), so
this is purely swapping the launch primitive: every startApplication() caller --
the window manager, the autostart phases, and the saved-session restore -- now
forks the binary directly.

(An earlier revision also hard-coded kdesktop/kicker launches in
restoreSession()/startDefaultSession() as a workaround for the autostart-phase
DCOP handshake not completing.  That handshake now runs, so the hard-coded
launches raced a second copy of kicker/kdesktop against the autostart's own and
have been removed -- the autostart/restore is the single launch path.)

--- a/ksmserver/server.cpp
+++ b/ksmserver/server.cpp
@@ -118,10 +118,26 @@
     }
     int n = command.count();
     TQCString app = command[0].latin1();
-    TQValueList<TQCString> argList;
-    for ( int i=1; i < n; i++)
-       argList.append( TQCString(command[i].latin1()));
-    DCOPRef( launcher ).send( "exec_blind", app, DCOPArg( argList, "TQValueList<TQCString>" ) );
+
+    /* substrate: launch session apps (the window manager, panel, desktop, ...)
+     * directly with fork()+execvp() rather than the async DCOP exec_blind() to
+     * tdelauncher, whose start_service path does not reliably bring up kdesktop
+     * here.  Inter-app DCOP still works (the af_unix SO_PEERCRED fix); this only
+     * swaps the launch primitive.  Binaries are on $PATH (/opt/trinity/bin). */
+    if ( fork() == 0 ) {
+        setsid();
+        char **argv = (char **) malloc( ( n + 1 ) * sizeof( char * ) );
+        argv[0] = strdup( app.data() );
+        for ( int i = 1; i < n; i++ )
+            argv[i] = strdup( command[i].latin1() );
+        argv[n] = 0;
+        execvp( argv[0], argv );
+        _exit( 127 );
+    }
 }

